MEMMGR compaction (345c/3439): guard the block-shift do-while loops so growing/shrinking the LAST
block (zero blocks to relocate) does not run the loop with count 0 -> out-of-bounds walk -> SIGSEGV.

The heap resize (345c) and realloc (3439) relocate the blocks that come after the resized one:
`n = block_count - (index+1)` (backward, grow) or `block_count - index` (forward, shrink), then shift
`n` blocks.  When the resized block is the last one (index == count-1, e.g. the KDV chunk buffer which
is the last-allocated block) n == 0 -- nothing to move.  Ghidra rendered the shift as a bare do-while
(`do { ... } while (iVarN != 0)`), which executes once even when the count is 0, then decrements past
zero and walks the per-block tables ([0x28ac]/[0x2bcc]/[0x2a3c]) out of bounds -> deref of a garbage
key -> SIGSEGV.  The companion prep routine 3584 already guards the same walk (`if (count < i+1 ||
iVar4 == 0) return`); the caller's copy loop must too.  Guard each of the four compaction loops with
its shift count so a 0-count resize is an in-place no-op (the faithful intent: shift N blocks, N=0 =>
none).  asm ref: 345c 0x34fa..0x3554 (mov esi,[2f60]; inc esi; mov ebp,[2f54]; sub ebp,esi; ...; dec
ebp; jne) -- the count is (count-(index+1)); for the last block that is 0.
